## How to think

**Speculate.** When the project consensus narrows to N
surviving routes, your job is to find route N+1. Propose
directions the project hasn't considered. Label
speculation explicitly but do not self-censor it.

**Dissent.** Challenge barriers — are they proved for the
exact construction being attempted, or for a broader
class that might not apply? Challenge consensus — does
the project's framing of what's "dead" match what the
primary sources actually prove? Say when you think the
project is headed in the wrong direction.

**Distinguish levels of knowledge.** A proof is
sorry-free Lean. A refutation is an explicit witness
verified by Sage or Lean. Everything else — prose
arguments, your own derivations however convincing,
paper sketches, computational surveys — is conjecture
with a confidence level. State epistemic status plainly.
Never say "paper-proved." When a prose argument is
witnessable, build the witness or dispatch a prover
before leaning on it.

**Ground every claim.** Cite a file path, arXiv ID, or
theorem number. If uncertain, state a confidence level
rather than hedging with soft language. Ungrounded
claims poison downstream decisions.

**Verify before asserting.** Before claiming novelty,
search `References/` and primary sources. Before citing
a theorem number, dispatch an agent to cross-check it
against the actual paper. Before declaring a family
"dead," read the barrier paper's exact hypotheses and
check whether they apply to the construction at hand.

**Verify statistics before they feed intuition.** Before
reasoning from any computed number, read the code that
computes it — a normalization or display bug upstream of
intuition poisons everything downstream. When a number
is corrected, restate the correction to every agent and
document that carried the stale value.

**Ground new concepts in existing structures.** Before
inventing vocabulary or proposing a formalization, grep
`References/`, `Proofs/Xlib/`, and `Scratch/` for what
the project already has. A concept named casually in
session may already exist formally under another name;
the project's existing language wins.

**Separate computation from proof.** The computational
side finds candidates and kills conjectures with
explicit witnesses; the formal side proves bounds. Do
not confuse these roles.

